Every element is a box. From the inside out it has content, padding (space inside the border), a border, and margin (space outside the border that pushes other boxes away).

```css
.card {
  width: 200px;
  padding: 16px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  margin: 24px;
}
```

Flexbox lays items out in a single row or column. Set `display: flex` on the parent; the children become flex items.

```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;  /* along the main axis (the row) */
  align-items: center;             /* across it (vertically in a row) */
  gap: 16px;
}
```

`justify-content` aligns items along the main axis, which is horizontal for `flex-direction: row`. `align-items` aligns them on the cross axis. Swapping the two is one of the most common flexbox mix-ups.

CSS grid places items in rows and columns at the same time, which suits page layouts and card galleries.

```css
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);  /* three equal columns */
  gap: 16px;
}
```

A navbar is usually a flexbox job (one row of links); a gallery of cards is usually a grid job.

A function is a reusable block of code. You define it once and call it with parentheses, passing arguments that fill its parameters.

```javascript
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
console.log(add(2, 3));   // 5
```

`return` sends a value back to the caller and ends the function. A function without `return` gives back `undefined`.

Arrow functions are a shorter syntax that is common for small functions and callbacks. With a single expression and no braces, the value is returned automatically.

```javascript
const double = (x) => x * 2;
const greet = (name) => {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
};
```

If you add braces to an arrow function you must write `return` yourself; forgetting it is a common reason a function returns `undefined`.

TypeScript is JavaScript with types. You can describe what kind of value a variable or parameter should hold, and the TypeScript compiler checks it before the code runs.

```typescript
let count: number = 0;
function greet(name: string): string {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}
greet(42);   // error: Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.
```

These errors appear in your editor or when you compile; the types are removed from the JavaScript that actually runs.

An `interface` (or a `type` alias) describes the shape of an object: which properties it has and their types. A question mark marks an optional property.

```typescript
interface Learner {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  nickname?: string;   // optional
}
const sara: Learner = { name: "Sara", age: 15 };
```

Leaving out a required property, or adding one that the interface does not declare, is reported as an error.

A union type allows one of several types, written with `|`. Before using a value as one specific type, check which one you have; TypeScript narrows the type inside the check.

```typescript
function show(id: number | string) {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    return id.toUpperCase();   // id is a string here
  }
  return id.toFixed(0);        // id is a number here
}
```
